#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133689 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133873 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df300955-830a-5347-9aeb-2b2b25f865c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a One-Dimensional Subspace"}] :question/id #uuid "18068394-b64b-5bc8-a2da-b0db4085ecba" :question/math-academy-id "e-8729" :question/problem "Let $V = \\Bbb R_1[t],$ the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $1$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n\\langle p(t),q(t) \\rangle = p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nfind the orthogonal projection of $p(t)=-5t-3$ onto the subspace spanned by the polynomial $q(t) =t-1.$" :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $p(t)$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{ q(t) \\}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) \n= \\dfrac{\\langle{p(t),q(t)}\\rangle}{\\langle{q(t),q(t)}\\rangle}\\,q(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1) \\\\  & =(-5t-3){|}_{0}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{0}+(-5t-3){|}_{1}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot (-1)+(-8)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =3+0 \\\\  & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨q(t),q(t)⟩ & =q(0)q(0)+q(1)q(1) \\\\  & =(t-1){|}_{0}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{0}+(t-1){|}_{1}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =1+0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) & =\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{1}\\cdot (t-1) \\\\  & =3(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133690 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133874 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "39692b40-7d68-5ae5-9fe1-0f8b0b2e0d77" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "4e4a5db0-2ea0-542e-abdd-c5b3abfe2134" :question/math-academy-id "e-8730" :question/problem "Let $V = \\mathcal{C}[-1,1],$ the vector space of continuous functions over $[-1,1]$, and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle =\\int_{-1}^{1} x(t) y(t) \\, \\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf_1(t)=1,\\quad f_2(t)=\\sin \\pi t, \\quad g(t)=6t^2\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}\\{f_1(t), f_2(t)\\}$ given that $f_1(t) \\perp f_2(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{f_1(t)\\}$ equals $2.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{f_1(t),f_2(t)\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,g(t) \n= \\textrm{proj}_{f_1(t)}\\,g(t) + \\textrm{proj}_{f_2(t)}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that $\\textrm{proj}_{f_1(t)}\\,g(t) = 2,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{f_2(t)\\}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{f_2(t)}\\,g(t) \n= \\dfrac{\\langle{g(t),f_2(t)}\\rangle}{\\langle{f_2(t),f_2(t)}\\rangle}\\,f_2(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}g(t){f}_{2}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}6{t}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}6{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\sin (\\pi t)$ is an odd function, while $6t^2$ is even. Hence, their product $6 t^2 \\sin (\\pi t)$ must be an odd function. Now, since we have an integral over the interval $[-1,1],$ which is symmetric about the origin, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^{1}   6t^2 \\sin (\\pi t) \\, \\text{d}t = 0.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{f_2(t)}\\,g(t) = \\dfrac{0}{\\langle{f_2(t),f_2(t)}\\rangle} \\cdot  \\sin \\pi t = 0.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =2+0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133691 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133875 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "25e84fac-02c3-5111-98b3-bc67fe1ec9ab" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace"}] :question/id #uuid "d7541321-4042-54af-9b64-59f255a6f1a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-8733" :question/problem "Let $V = \\Bbb R_1[t],$ the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $1$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n\\langle p(t), q(t) \\rangle = p(0)q(0) + p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $p(t) = 4t + 2$ and the subspace spanned by $q(t) = t$ with respect to the inner product." :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $p(t)$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{ q(t) \\}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) & =\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1)}{q(0)^{2}+q(1)^{2}}\\cdot q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{(2)(0)+(6)(1)}{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\cdot t \\\\  & =6t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe distance between $p(t) = 4t + 2$ and the subspace spanned by $q(t) = t$ with respect to our inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖p(t)-{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖(4t+2)-6t‖ \\\\  & =‖-2t+2‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨-2t+2,\\text{ }-2t+2⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨-2t+2,\\text{ }-2t+2⟩ & =(-2t+2){|}_{0}\\cdot (-2t+2){|}_{0}+(-2t+2){|}_{1}\\cdot (-2t+2){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-2(0)+2)^{2}+(-2(1)+2)^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}+{0}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d = \\sqrt{4} = 2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240903 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133873 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df300955-830a-5347-9aeb-2b2b25f865c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a One-Dimensional Subspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00fe2cae-05df-5f76-876b-4be60d9d76f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(t+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238088} {:answer/id #uuid "7e0f060c-132f-5025-97bf-ffe5449b1d33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(t+1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239151} {:answer/id #uuid "96b003aa-c4e4-5c2a-b5a4-c93e075d8dab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239357} {:answer/id #uuid "d06c1a67-9285-5164-91a4-b27576af86e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239881} {:answer/id #uuid "f0324f0b-62b0-552c-b99e-c8adb90ceb22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3t+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00fe2cae-05df-5f76-876b-4be60d9d76f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(t+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238088} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b216aab-b0e0-5927-8b86-d046fffbad9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240376}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4d406e5c-2737-5c6c-bad0-bb58ddb067d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-104586" :question/problem "Let $V={R}_{2}[t],$ the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $2$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nfind the orthogonal projection of $p(t)=5{t}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by $q(t)=t+1.$" :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $p(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{q(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t)=\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1) \\\\  & =(5{t}^{2}){|}_{-1}\\,\\cdot \\,(t\\,+\\,1){|}_{-1}+(5{t}^{2}){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot \\,(t\\,+\\,1){|}_{0}+(5{t}^{2}){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot \\,(t\\,+\\,1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 1+5\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =0+0+10 \\\\  & =10,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨q(t),q(t)⟩ & =q(-1)q(-1)+q(0)q(0)+q(1)q(1) \\\\  & =(t\\,+\\,1){|}_{-1}\\,\\cdot \\,(t\\,+\\,1){|}_{-1}+(t\\,+\\,1){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot \\,(t\\,+\\,1){|}_{0}+(t\\,+\\,1){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot \\,(t\\,+\\,1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =0+1+4 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) & =\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{5}\\cdot (t+1) \\\\  & =2(t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240904 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133873 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df300955-830a-5347-9aeb-2b2b25f865c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a One-Dimensional Subspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f6f16ad-810b-5af6-81b5-41c7f62bfc97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3t-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238607} {:answer/id #uuid "a2c56750-762d-5579-b177-e16cbf7d9a02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(t-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239470} {:answer/id #uuid "b2a05d0c-6b28-5f09-97e7-d2f65f26077e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239613} {:answer/id #uuid "baeada88-c22e-532f-9d14-7c176a626e8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239680} {:answer/id #uuid "cac83832-9ca0-5672-b3e5-6c4b4303ca4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(t-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cac83832-9ca0-5672-b3e5-6c4b4303ca4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(t-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239834} :answer-field/id #uuid "376aad8b-594e-5b5b-8e27-d900a1b34d59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "99c91096-e517-53ed-91eb-c1bbb59a1a8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-104587" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the orthogonal projection of $f(t)=12({t}^{2}-t)$ onto the subspace spanned by $g(t)=t-1.$" :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $f(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{g(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t)=\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}12({t}^{2}-t)\\cdot (t-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(12{t}^{3}-24{t}^{2}+12t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[3{t}^{4}-8{t}^{3}+6{t}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(3-8+6)-0 \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}-2t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}+t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}-1+1)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})}\\cdot (t-1) \\\\  & =3(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240950 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133874 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "39692b40-7d68-5ae5-9fe1-0f8b0b2e0d77" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2079d458-9330-562a-9453-cf36fc05dba6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238336} {:answer/id #uuid "82a8422b-0a62-5625-835a-3dc34096151b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8t+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239191} {:answer/id #uuid "c00cb18f-5e51-54da-87e5-e8e12ed425e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4t-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239726} {:answer/id #uuid "e939e30a-147b-5be2-8ba3-ecf58b548d7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8t-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240108} {:answer/id #uuid "ff4ff528-7f94-5138-9525-dd2f5f86d0a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4t+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240318}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c00cb18f-5e51-54da-87e5-e8e12ed425e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4t-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239726} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e86f954-7d54-5afe-9619-a1f0a8f4bd59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240558}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e18df242-9d5f-58ab-883f-e2f596589751" :question/math-academy-id "q-104871" :question/problem "Let $V={R}_{1}[t],$ the vector space of real polynomials of degree at most $1,$ and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1).\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=2t,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=t-1,\\,q(t)=4t-3\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)}$ given that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{p}_{1}(t)}$ equals $t.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,q(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)=t,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{p}_{2}(t)}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t)=\\frac{⟨q(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩}{⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩}\\,{p}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨q(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =q(0){p}_{2}(0)+q(1){p}_{2}(1) \\\\  & =(4t-3){|}_{0}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{0}+(4t-3){|}_{1}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={p}_{2}(0){p}_{2}(0)+{p}_{2}(1){p}_{2}(1) \\\\  & =(t-1)^{2}{|}_{0}+(t-1)^{2}{|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t) & =\\frac{3}{1}\\cdot (t-1)=3t-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,q(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =t+(3t-3) \\\\  & =4t-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240955 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133874 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "39692b40-7d68-5ae5-9fe1-0f8b0b2e0d77" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1caca5e3-ca04-5633-9793-2c76970948cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238295} {:answer/id #uuid "31a76e28-eba0-58a6-93c0-42ef6d756298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}+t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238487} {:answer/id #uuid "39e5f7d7-52f3-53e8-83e1-19f3605d9145" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238555} {:answer/id #uuid "6535292f-9a6c-5ca9-aa33-f12952102aa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238940} {:answer/id #uuid "bd6e085f-d8c7-572b-8a82-cf7a4c7698ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239706}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd6e085f-d8c7-572b-8a82-cf7a4c7698ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239706} :answer-field/id #uuid "1047e2e9-2fa7-5303-9790-543a7c6c55a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e0a44c8c-788c-548d-8b09-deb0ad2858dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-104894" :question/problem "Let $V={R}_{2}[t],$ the vector space of real polynomials of degree at most $2,$ and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1).\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=t,\\,{p}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}-1,\\,q(t)={t}^{2}+t\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)}$ given that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{p}_{2}(t)}$ equals $0.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,q(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t)=0,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{p}_{1}(t)}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)=\\frac{⟨q(t),{p}_{1}(t)⟩}{⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{1}(t)⟩}\\,{p}_{1}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨q(t),{p}_{1}(t)⟩ & =q(-1){p}_{1}(-1)+q(0){p}_{1}(0)+q(1){p}_{1}(1) \\\\  & =({t}^{2}\\,+\\,t){|}_{-1}\\,\\,\\cdot \\,(t){|}_{-1}+({t}^{2}\\,+\\,t){|}_{0}\\,\\,\\cdot \\,(t){|}_{0}+({t}^{2}\\,+\\,t){|}_{1}\\,\\,\\cdot \\,(t){|}_{1} \\\\  & =0\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{1}(t)⟩ & ={p}_{1}(-1){p}_{1}(-1)+{p}_{1}(0){p}_{1}(0)+{p}_{1}(1){p}_{1}(1) \\\\  & =(t)^{2}{|}_{-1}+(t)^{2}{|}_{0}+(t)^{2}{|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t) & =\\frac{2}{2}\\cdot t=t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFinally,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,q(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =t+0 \\\\  & =t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240962 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133873 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df300955-830a-5347-9aeb-2b2b25f865c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a One-Dimensional Subspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c262ec4-1077-5eba-960a-29a37e4d49e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{t}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238860} {:answer/id #uuid "6548612e-e066-56ee-8307-5a72d4c5b46c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{t}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238941} {:answer/id #uuid "7ba83d61-3ae8-59ae-a55d-e72b143845a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{t}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239122} {:answer/id #uuid "d03f29ea-0758-56ab-b50f-22810c22f6b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10{t}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239877} {:answer/id #uuid "fc9c38fc-ca96-579e-85b3-bed4f624e931" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{t}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240287}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d03f29ea-0758-56ab-b50f-22810c22f6b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10{t}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239877} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdce64c6-9a10-5438-b66b-cee58c936ae5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240775}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6a0afbf2-8aa8-5003-9b37-01bec551a919" :question/math-academy-id "q-104936" :question/problem "Let $V=C[-1,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[-1,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the orthogonal projection of $f(t)={t}^{3}+10{t}^{2}+2$ onto the subspace spanned by $g(t)=5{t}^{2}+1.$" :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $f(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{g(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t)=\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({t}^{3}+10{t}^{2}+2)\\cdot (5{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(5{t}^{5}+50{t}^{4}+{t}^{3}+20{t}^{2}+2)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{5}{6}{t}^{6}+10{t}^{5}+\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{4}+\\frac{20}{3}{t}^{3}+2t]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{6}+10+\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{20}{3}+2)-(\\frac{5}{6}-10+\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{20}{3}-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{112}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(5{t}^{2}+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(25{t}^{4}+10{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[5{t}^{5}+\\frac{10}{3}{t}^{3}+t]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(5+\\frac{10}{3}+1)-(-5-\\frac{10}{3}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{56}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{112}{3})}{(\\frac{56}{3})}\\cdot (5{t}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =2(5{t}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =10{t}^{2}+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240978 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133874 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "39692b40-7d68-5ae5-9fe1-0f8b0b2e0d77" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c5caa67-c911-5ad0-8f2b-aa4c00b90cc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10{t}^{3}+3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238292} {:answer/id #uuid "32e18caa-4580-5ff6-9760-b3dcb3dac655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238498} {:answer/id #uuid "8e55c40f-86f3-5748-8fdb-5fa0410ee712" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239290} {:answer/id #uuid "e76649ac-05ad-5f2e-a3c3-ed56c8b3436f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240092} {:answer/id #uuid "f85b144c-8163-5666-ae5c-309d42c47eb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}+6t+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240253}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32e18caa-4580-5ff6-9760-b3dcb3dac655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238498} :answer-field/id #uuid "f02b5045-4774-5001-9f14-da28bf31cb04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240751}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2794de9d-8dc5-594e-8a2b-e907f92bf64b" :question/math-academy-id "q-105100" :question/problem "Let $V=C[-1,1],$ the vector space of continuous functions over $[-1,1]$, and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$ \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=3t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}+1,\\,g(t)=10{t}^{3}\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ given that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t)}$ equals $6t.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)=6t,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{2}(t)}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\,{f}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}g(t){f}_{2}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}10{t}^{3}\\cdot ({t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}10{t}^{3}({t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $10{t}^{3}$ is an odd function, while ${t}^{2}+1$ is even. Hence, their product $10{t}^{3}({t}^{2}+1)$ must be an odd function. Now, since we have an integral over the interval $[-1,1],$ which is symmetric about the origin, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}10{t}^{3}({t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t=0.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{0}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\cdot ({t}^{2}+1)=0.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =6t+0 \\\\  & =6t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240983 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133874 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "39692b40-7d68-5ae5-9fe1-0f8b0b2e0d77" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09b29cd5-55b5-5722-ba90-23f769345723" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238150} {:answer/id #uuid "5bc19ba5-b098-5af2-b759-18b7d61fa0df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238854} {:answer/id #uuid "64e8e822-ab38-5fcf-a7cb-162397992404" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238937} {:answer/id #uuid "9cd846ec-dcb9-5576-8a82-5cbd0e68c90d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239418} {:answer/id #uuid "a19047b8-6624-5f0c-b217-a2d6a5d4fcd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239462}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64e8e822-ab38-5fcf-a7cb-162397992404" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238937} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5a8c5d5-fcb2-5bdd-8555-14fa5c61c1d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "645a82fe-e42f-5dc9-9e79-d8bd0747a894" :question/math-academy-id "q-105136" :question/problem "Let $V=C[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the vector space of continuous functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$, and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,g(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ given that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t)}$ equals $5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{2}(t)}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\,{f}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(t){f}_{2}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t-4{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ is an odd function, while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is even. Hence, their product $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ must be an odd function. Now, since we have an integral over the interval $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ which is symmetric about the origin, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =-4{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) & =\\frac{-4{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =\\frac{-4{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241003 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133874 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "39692b40-7d68-5ae5-9fe1-0f8b0b2e0d77" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a9db971-884b-5cf9-9a84-c91c9fa4fa01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238276} {:answer/id #uuid "39658250-b0fe-59bb-a334-a4e517b0d75e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238550} {:answer/id #uuid "880b7d31-ec04-56db-ad2a-f754621f1b8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239237} {:answer/id #uuid "b1b15087-e383-5255-8efc-84ad43c168b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239607} {:answer/id #uuid "cd180c30-2f84-5a24-af62-8965f36db06d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1b15087-e383-5255-8efc-84ad43c168b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239607} :answer-field/id #uuid "51c982ce-3e72-5b74-bcb0-6eb543685681" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240480}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2bf55fb2-ca86-5707-a52a-d891899878f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-105186" :question/problem "Let $V=C[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the vector space of continuous functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$, and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=2,\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+6\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ given that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t)}$ equals $6.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)=6,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{2}(t)}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\,{f}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(t){f}_{2}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+6)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t-2{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+6{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ is an odd function, while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is even. Hence, their product $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ must be an odd function. Now, since we have integrals over the interval $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ which is symmetric about the origin, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =-2{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) & =\\frac{-2{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =\\frac{-2{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =6-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241059 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133875 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "25e84fac-02c3-5111-98b3-bc67fe1ec9ab" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "694bbe7e-1033-5685-8db7-c579e340a4af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238969} {:answer/id #uuid "7187a2ab-aec2-5516-9817-82c6b1e6f0ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239028} {:answer/id #uuid "98f9f087-a4cb-5b2b-ba88-6c34977c1ab6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239383} {:answer/id #uuid "d0b7eedf-e87f-53c1-a7c0-62ebe727a2fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239882} {:answer/id #uuid "f69f76e8-1b93-5cd1-b517-2cdfc888efb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7187a2ab-aec2-5516-9817-82c6b1e6f0ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239028} :answer-field/id #uuid "eabf99e8-310f-5528-b7c0-2d81c99774f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240742}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a70f1b99-0d4b-5ef2-9e7e-0dc7f6d78417" :question/math-academy-id "q-105461" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $f(t)=3t$ and $g(t)=-3t+3$ with respect to the inner product." :question/worked-solution "The distance between $f(t)=3t$ and $g(t)=-3t+3$ with respect to the given inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖f(t)-g(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖3t-(-3t+3)‖ \\\\  & =‖6t-3‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨6t-3,6t-3⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨6t-3,6t-3⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(6t-3)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(36{t}^{2}-36t+9)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[12{t}^{3}-18{t}^{2}+9t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(12-18+9)-0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241065 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133875 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "25e84fac-02c3-5111-98b3-bc67fe1ec9ab" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48a7b1d1-9e0c-5434-b07f-cf7c34c58455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238689} {:answer/id #uuid "6cb047b4-005a-5f38-99ac-00f3ebfaed46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238993} {:answer/id #uuid "81a1bedc-3c44-57ff-97aa-f3479fb93f61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239182} {:answer/id #uuid "98c7b5d0-5735-504e-86cc-9c4d8759a4d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{60}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239380} {:answer/id #uuid "f8f30da2-e1c2-5b02-ad11-c9204c994d49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240257}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8f30da2-e1c2-5b02-ad11-c9204c994d49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240257} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0036792-c1cf-521a-8145-d8b6a16d1265" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240750}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a014caff-6523-5f53-81ab-1541b84035b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-105488" :question/problem "Let $V={R}_{2}[t],$ the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $2$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $p(t)=5t$ and the subspace spanned by $q(t)=t-1$ with respect to the inner product." :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $p(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{q(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) & =\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1)}{q(-1)q(-1)+q(0)q(0)+q(1)q(1)}\\cdot q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\sum }_{i\\in {-1,0,1}}(5t){|}_{t=i}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{t=i}}{{\\sum }_{i\\in {-1,0,1}}(t-1){|}_{t=i}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{t=i}}\\cdot (t-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-5)\\cdot (-2)+0\\cdot (-1)+5\\cdot 0}{(-2)\\cdot (-2)+(-1)\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 0}\\cdot (t-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{5}\\cdot (t-1) \\\\  & =2(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe distance between $p(t)=5t$ and the subspace spanned by $q(t)=t-1$ with respect to our inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖p(t)-{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖5t-2(t-1)‖ \\\\  & =‖3t+2‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨3t+2,3t+2⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨3t+2,3t+2⟩ & ={\\sum }_{i\\in {-1,0,1}}(3t+2){|}_{t=i}\\cdot (3t+2){|}_{t=i} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 2+5\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =1+4+25 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{30}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241072 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133875 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "25e84fac-02c3-5111-98b3-bc67fe1ec9ab" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c177ebb-74f2-5df6-ad9b-462f191cb199" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238448} {:answer/id #uuid "4130a5d7-8c4d-5063-abeb-e26e9d5e5993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238622} {:answer/id #uuid "7b47653c-8ce4-54e4-a40b-0b94964d09dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239117} {:answer/id #uuid "e86b696c-29ba-5e81-84bb-95c894903bb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240100} {:answer/id #uuid "edae4073-9987-58fc-ad81-166cfa56cdaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240148}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edae4073-9987-58fc-ad81-166cfa56cdaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240148} :answer-field/id #uuid "7465bcae-e6d9-5d93-9ec3-ebbbe750de79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "78c3ca4e-1ab2-52da-b32e-1ddeb67471e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-105498" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $f(t)=8t-3$ and $g(t)=2t-1$ with respect to the inner product." :question/worked-solution "The distance between $f(t)=8t-3$ and $g(t)=2t-1$ with respect to the given inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖f(t)-g(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖8t-3-(2t-1)‖ \\\\  & =‖6t-2‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨6t-2,6t-2⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨6t-2,6t-2⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(6t-2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(36{t}^{2}-24t+4)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[12{t}^{3}-12{t}^{2}+4t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(12-12+4)-0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{4}=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375652 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133875 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "25e84fac-02c3-5111-98b3-bc67fe1ec9ab" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69984854-c740-507d-a2b8-fdb5e8a42fe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375654} {:answer/id #uuid "65e90a69-6ff8-5d0d-ac71-6f870d3f72c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375655} {:answer/id #uuid "19d6b488-912b-552c-9eb4-cfe91a62376d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375656} {:answer/id #uuid "d3c16f29-9e9a-56f7-987c-bcfe8d3996cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375657} {:answer/id #uuid "b69f5f8e-7f81-5690-b8cf-f05a442074d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b69f5f8e-7f81-5690-b8cf-f05a442074d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375658} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5eb4129-4179-5cad-b537-5964562a2c96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375653}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ec6ca611-cb70-5ddf-8aeb-37ae54b275e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-105510" :question/problem "Let $V={R}_{2}[t],$ the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $2$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $p(t)=5{t}^{2}-5$ and the subspace spanned by $q(t)=t+1$ with respect to the inner product." :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $p(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{q(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) & =\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1)}{q(-1)q(-1)+q(0)q(0)+q(1)q(1)}\\cdot q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\sum }_{i\\in {-1,0,1}}(5{t}^{2}-5){|}_{t=i}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{t=i}}{{\\sum }_{i\\in {-1,0,1}}(t+1){|}_{t=i}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{t=i}}\\cdot (t+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot 0+(-5)\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 2}{0\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2}\\cdot (t+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{-5}{5}\\cdot (t+1) \\\\  & =-t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe distance between $p(t)=5{t}^{2}-5$ and the subspace spanned by $q(t)=t+1$ with respect to our inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖p(t)-{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖5{t}^{2}-5-(-t-1)‖ \\\\  & =‖5{t}^{2}+t-4‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨5{t}^{2}+t-4,5{t}^{2}+t-4⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨5{t}^{2}+t-4,5{t}^{2}+t-4⟩ & ={\\sum }_{i\\in {-1,0,1}}(5{t}^{2}+t-4){|}_{t=i}\\cdot (5{t}^{2}+t-4){|}_{t=i} \\\\  & =(0)\\cdot (0)+(-4)\\cdot (-4)+2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =0+16+4 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{20}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241074 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133875 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "25e84fac-02c3-5111-98b3-bc67fe1ec9ab" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36d086e7-9a00-54ba-a345-73d4f86ae07e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238528} {:answer/id #uuid "4b4f0cb7-0675-5d69-8e34-a9010b2385ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238704} {:answer/id #uuid "82b79539-c633-51dc-94f9-26b7bd83dad4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239192} {:answer/id #uuid "c976c00b-2eb7-5e8a-a3d2-f96e04c9ee69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239824} {:answer/id #uuid "f7788d12-3ed7-512d-b2fa-46025ca6abef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240242}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36d086e7-9a00-54ba-a345-73d4f86ae07e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238528} :answer-field/id #uuid "10f5b310-4ec3-5cee-aa2c-6dc0e3e887bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240356}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c26c2acf-e85e-5f98-8d84-d3505431183c" :question/math-academy-id "q-105511" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $f(t)=7t$ and the subspace spanned by $g(t)=t-2$ with respect to the inner product." :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $f(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{g(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\int }_{0}^{1}7t\\cdot (t-2)\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot (t-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\int }_{0}^{1}(7{t}^{2}-14t)\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}-4t+4)\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot (t-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{[\\frac{7}{3}{t}^{3}-7{t}^{2}]_{0}^{1}}{[\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}+4t]_{0}^{1}}\\cdot (t-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{14}{3})}{(\\frac{7}{3})}\\cdot (t-2) \\\\  & =-2t+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe distance between $f(t)=7t$ and the subspace spanned by $g(t)=t-2$ with respect to our inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖f(t)-{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖7t-(-2t+4)‖ \\\\  & =‖9t-4‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨9t-4,9t-4⟩}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨9t-4,9t-4⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(9t-4)\\cdot (9t-4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(9t-4)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(81{t}^{2}-72t+16)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(27{t}^{3}-36{t}^{2}+16)\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{7}.$"}]}
